1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,696
"A contributor we have never heard of opened
a pull request on our public evaluation

2
00:00:09,736 --> 00:00:14,558
library. Eight hours later there were
malicious versions of our package on the

3
00:00:14,598 --> 00:00:19,046
registry, published by our own release
workflow. No password was stolen.

4
00:00:19,086 --> 00:00:19,358
How?"

5
00:00:19,698 --> 00:00:24,839
That's the shape of a real incident of the
eleventh of May 2026, and video 162 goes

6
00:00:24,879 --> 00:00:28,834
through it. Notice one thing: each
step was documented behavior.

7
00:00:28,874 --> 00:00:34,016
A workflow trigger, the event that starts
an automated run, that runs with the base

8
00:00:34,056 --> 00:00:37,886
repository's privileges. A checkout
of the contributor's code.

9
00:00:37,926 --> 00:00:42,942
A cache, a directory one run stores for a
later run, shared with the release job.

10
00:00:42,982 --> 00:00:48,124
A release job that could request a publishing
identity, the credential it publishes

11
00:00:48,164 --> 00:00:49,185
the package with.

12
00:00:49,525 --> 00:00:52,865
A CTO who asks "how?" doesn't
want a list of settings.

13
00:00:52,905 --> 00:00:58,124
They want to know which line of which file
gave an outsider code execution next to a

14
00:00:58,164 --> 00:01:03,383
credential, why the platform allowed it,
what the smallest change is that closes it,

15
00:01:03,423 --> 00:01:08,580
and how you'll know it stays closed. A
smaller puzzle for today: one workflow file,

16
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:12,837
copied unchanged into a second
repository, where one step now fails.

17
00:01:12,877 --> 00:01:13,713
Hold on to it.

18
00:01:15,553 --> 00:01:19,561
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

19
00:01:19,601 --> 00:01:24,008
This is Part 7: security. A word
about what this part is and isn't.

20
00:01:24,348 --> 00:01:29,084
It is defensive. The chapter says so in
its second paragraph: it shows unsafe

21
00:01:29,124 --> 00:01:34,232
workflow patterns next to their fixes, at
the level of detail GitHub's own security

22
00:01:34,272 --> 00:01:38,946
documentation uses, so that you can
review and harden your team's workflows.

23
00:01:38,986 --> 00:01:42,854
It contains no working attack.
The same holds for these videos.

24
00:01:43,194 --> 00:01:48,011
And the evidence statement, as for Part
6. Nothing in this chapter was run on

25
00:01:48,051 --> 00:01:53,246
GitHub. The transcripts show plain Git and
grep, a command that prints the matching

26
00:01:53,286 --> 00:01:57,409
lines of a file, on the course's
workflow files. The five files in

27
00:01:57,449 --> 00:02:01,635
workflows/vulnerable/ are teaching
material with faults on purpose.

28
00:02:01,675 --> 00:02:06,745
They were assembled from documented syntax
and parse-checked, and never executed.

29
00:02:06,785 --> 00:02:09,205
Never copy them into a real repository.

30
00:02:09,545 --> 00:02:14,718
Five words before we start. A workflow is
a file that says: when this event happens,

31
00:02:14,758 --> 00:02:19,310
run these jobs. A job is a list of
steps on one fresh machine, the runner.

32
00:02:19,350 --> 00:02:24,337
A token is a credential: a string that
stands for an account with a subset of its

33
00:02:24,377 --> 00:02:29,488
rights. A pull request proposes merging a
branch into yours, and a fork is a second

34
00:02:29,528 --> 00:02:31,846
repository on GitHub, made from yours.

35
00:02:32,186 --> 00:02:37,034
This first video gives you the model in five
parts, and then takes the first part in

36
00:02:37,074 --> 00:02:41,923
full: the job token and the permissions key.
The demonstration is an inventory: five

37
00:02:41,963 --> 00:02:46,346
files you've never seen, and four grep
commands that tell you where to look.

38
00:02:48,186 --> 00:02:49,764
Here's what you'll walk away with.

39
00:02:50,104 --> 00:02:51,516
After this video you can:

40
00:02:51,856 --> 00:02:56,512
describe the security model of a workflow
run in the five parts the section names;

41
00:02:56,852 --> 00:03:01,585
say what the job token can do when a workflow
has no permissions key and what that

42
00:03:01,625 --> 00:03:02,225
depends on;

43
00:03:02,565 --> 00:03:04,992
write least-privilege
permissions for a job;

44
00:03:05,332 --> 00:03:10,386
inventory the triggers, permissions, actions
and secrets of a set of workflow files

45
00:03:10,426 --> 00:03:11,613
with local commands.

46
00:03:13,453 --> 00:03:18,213
"Whose code runs in this job, on whose
text does it operate, and what can the job

47
00:03:18,253 --> 00:03:18,627
reach?"

48
00:03:18,967 --> 00:03:23,560
The textbook says the whole chapter reduces
to that one question, asked of every

49
00:03:23,600 --> 00:03:28,250
job. If the answer to the first two parts
is "somebody outside the team", and the

50
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:33,057
answer to the third is anything other than
"nothing", you've found a vulnerability,

51
00:03:33,097 --> 00:03:34,967
a weakness an outsider could use.

52
00:03:35,307 --> 00:03:40,301
In one sentence. A workflow run is a program
that GitHub starts on your behalf, with

53
00:03:40,341 --> 00:03:44,915
a credential for your repository in its
pocket, and its safety depends on who

54
00:03:44,955 --> 00:03:47,491
controls the program's
code and its inputs.

55
00:03:47,831 --> 00:03:49,428
Precisely: five parts.

56
00:03:49,768 --> 00:03:54,896
One. The job token. Each job receives a
GITHUB_TOKEN limited to the workflow's

57
00:03:54,936 --> 00:03:58,607
repository, and the permissions
key sets what it may do.

58
00:03:58,947 --> 00:04:04,364
Two. Fork pull requests. A pull_request
run from a fork gets a read-only token and

59
00:04:04,404 --> 00:04:08,291
no secrets, the encrypted values
kept in GitHub's settings.

60
00:04:08,331 --> 00:04:11,885
By default only first-time
contributors need approval.

61
00:04:12,225 --> 00:04:16,729
Three. Privileged triggers.
pull_request_target, and also workflow_run and

62
00:04:16,769 --> 00:04:20,659
issue_comment, run with the base
repository's token and secrets.

63
00:04:20,699 --> 00:04:24,466
The base repository is the one
that receives the pull request.

64
00:04:24,506 --> 00:04:28,519
These triggers are safe only while
they don't run the fork's code.

65
00:04:28,859 --> 00:04:33,615
Four. Expression injection. The dollar-brace
expression, a formula that Actions

66
00:04:33,655 --> 00:04:38,289
replaces with its value, is substituted
into the script text before the shell

67
00:04:38,329 --> 00:04:41,324
starts, so attacker-controlled
fields become code.

68
00:04:41,664 --> 00:04:47,384
Five. Third-party actions. An action is a
packaged step: someone else's code inside

69
00:04:47,424 --> 00:04:50,923
your job. It runs with the
job's token and secrets.

70
00:04:50,963 --> 00:04:54,739
A tag can be moved. Only a
full commit ID is immutable.

71
00:04:55,079 --> 00:04:58,312
The next videos take parts
two to five one at a time.

72
00:04:58,352 --> 00:05:02,943
And one statement about layers: nothing
in this model is a property of Git.

73
00:05:03,283 --> 00:05:06,911
Git contributes exactly two
facts that the model relies on.

74
00:05:06,951 --> 00:05:10,455
A tag, a name for one commit,
is a ref that can be moved.

75
00:05:10,495 --> 00:05:13,439
And a commit ID names
content that can't change.

76
00:05:13,779 --> 00:05:18,759
The job token. In one sentence: every job
gets its own short-lived token for the

77
00:05:18,799 --> 00:05:23,780
repository that contains the workflow,
and the permissions key decides what that

78
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:24,596
token may do.

79
00:05:24,936 --> 00:05:29,217
Precisely. The documentation: "At the
start of each workflow job, GitHub

80
00:05:29,257 --> 00:05:33,778
automatically creates a unique GITHUB_TOKEN
secret to use in your workflow."

81
00:05:33,818 --> 00:05:38,819
It's a GitHub App installation access token,
and its permissions "are limited to the

82
00:05:38,859 --> 00:05:43,260
repository that contains your workflow".
It expires when the job finishes.

83
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:46,981
On GitHub-hosted runners it can
live for a maximum of 6 hours.

84
00:05:47,321 --> 00:05:49,928
What the token may do is
decided in three layers.

85
00:05:50,268 --> 00:05:54,962
The repository or organization default:
the setting called "Workflow permissions".

86
00:05:55,002 --> 00:05:58,194
It decides what a workflow
with no permissions key gets.

87
00:05:58,234 --> 00:06:03,043
There are two options, quoted from the
documentation: "read and write access for all

88
00:06:03,083 --> 00:06:07,430
permissions", or read access for the
contents and packages permissions only.

89
00:06:07,770 --> 00:06:12,516
The workflow: the top-level permissions key
sets the token of every job that doesn't

90
00:06:12,556 --> 00:06:13,199
override it.

91
00:06:13,539 --> 00:06:17,826
The job: a permissions key under the
job sets the token of that job only.

92
00:06:18,166 --> 00:06:20,654
Three rules of the key
carry most of the weight.

93
00:06:20,994 --> 00:06:26,262
Rule one, quoted: "If you specify the
access for any of these permissions, all of

94
00:06:26,302 --> 00:06:31,308
those that are not specified are set to
none." So writing contents: read also

95
00:06:31,348 --> 00:06:35,371
removes issues, packages,
pull-requests and every other scope.

96
00:06:35,411 --> 00:06:37,730
A scope is one named area of access.

97
00:06:38,070 --> 00:06:42,868
Quick quiz. A workflow's only
permissions line is issues: write.

98
00:06:42,908 --> 00:06:46,874
Can its token read the
repository's contents? A, yes.

99
00:06:46,914 --> 00:06:48,310
B, no. Your answer?

100
00:06:53,150 --> 00:06:58,275
B. Naming one scope sets every other
one to none, reading contents included.

101
00:06:58,315 --> 00:07:01,061
Almost everyone expects A the first time.

102
00:07:01,401 --> 00:07:04,427
Rule two: permissions with an
empty map removes everything.

103
00:07:04,467 --> 00:07:06,818
read-all and write-all
are the two shorthands.

104
00:07:07,158 --> 00:07:12,640
Rule three: the scopes. On the first of
October 2026 the list has sixteen names,

105
00:07:12,680 --> 00:07:18,300
among them actions, attestations, checks,
contents, deployments, id-token, issues,

106
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:22,028
packages, pull-requests,
security-events and statuses.

107
00:07:22,068 --> 00:07:25,893
Each takes read, write or
none, and write includes read.

108
00:07:26,233 --> 00:07:31,445
Older behavior: a workflow without a
permissions key received a read-write token.

109
00:07:31,485 --> 00:07:36,761
Current behavior: the default is read-only
for new repositories, organizations and

110
00:07:36,801 --> 00:07:42,207
enterprises. Since the second of February
2023, and the changelog adds: "this change

111
00:07:42,247 --> 00:07:46,940
will not impact any existing enterprises,
organizations or repositories".

112
00:07:46,980 --> 00:07:52,062
Recommended: never rely on the default.
Declare permissions at the top of every

113
00:07:52,102 --> 00:07:57,119
workflow, because a repository created
before February 2023 may still have the

114
00:07:57,159 --> 00:08:02,177
permissive setting. The Nx project's
post-mortem, its published account of the

115
00:08:02,217 --> 00:08:05,548
incident, names exactly this
as one of three causes.

116
00:08:05,888 --> 00:08:09,663
So the answer to "what can the token
do when there is no permissions key?"

117
00:08:09,703 --> 00:08:13,838
is: it depends on a setting you can't see
in the file, and on when the repository

118
00:08:13,878 --> 00:08:14,457
was created.

119
00:08:18,297 --> 00:08:22,607
Read the comment in the second block.
The job-level block replaces the

120
00:08:22,647 --> 00:08:25,901
workflow-level one, so
contents is none for that job.

121
00:08:25,941 --> 00:08:29,381
That's correct here, because
the job checks nothing out.

122
00:08:29,421 --> 00:08:34,539
GitHub's guidance says the same in prose:
set the default permission to read access

123
00:08:34,579 --> 00:08:39,387
only for repository contents, and increase
it as required for individual jobs.

124
00:08:39,427 --> 00:08:44,421
That practice is called least privilege:
the smallest set of rights that does the

125
00:08:44,461 --> 00:08:44,669
job.

126
00:08:45,009 --> 00:08:48,148
Two properties limit what
a stolen job token can do.

127
00:08:48,188 --> 00:08:53,282
First: events triggered by the GITHUB_TOKEN
will not create a new workflow run, with

128
00:08:53,322 --> 00:08:57,621
workflow_dispatch and repository_dispatch
as the documented exceptions.

129
00:08:57,661 --> 00:09:02,572
The textbook warns that the exception isn't
academic: in the Nx incident a stolen

130
00:09:02,612 --> 00:09:06,179
read-write token was used to
dispatch the publish workflow.

131
00:09:06,219 --> 00:09:08,379
Second: the token dies with the job.

132
00:09:08,719 --> 00:09:13,684
Inside .git. The token also reaches Git.
The checkout action, which fetches the

133
00:09:13,724 --> 00:09:18,942
repository onto the runner, says in its
README: "The auth token is persisted in the

134
00:09:18,982 --> 00:09:23,884
local git config. This enables your
scripts to run authenticated git commands.

135
00:09:23,924 --> 00:09:28,889
The token is removed during post-job
cleanup. Set persist-credentials: false to

136
00:09:28,929 --> 00:09:29,459
opt-out."

137
00:09:29,799 --> 00:09:34,744
Since version 6 of the action the credential
is stored in a separate file under the

138
00:09:34,784 --> 00:09:39,668
runner's temporary directory. Either way,
until the job ends, every later step and

139
00:09:39,708 --> 00:09:44,593
every process it starts can run authenticated
Git commands against your repository

140
00:09:44,633 --> 00:09:49,577
with whatever contents permission the job
has. A job that only builds and tests has

141
00:09:49,617 --> 00:09:51,259
no reason to leave it there.

142
00:09:53,099 --> 00:09:58,107
Analogy for the model, from the textbook.
A contractor is let into your office each

143
00:09:58,147 --> 00:10:03,033
time a parcel arrives. The front desk gives
the contractor a badge, and the badge

144
00:10:03,073 --> 00:10:07,533
opens whatever doors the desk configured.
The contractor follows a written

145
00:10:07,573 --> 00:10:12,642
procedure, which is the workflow file. Uses
tools bought from other firms, which are

146
00:10:12,682 --> 00:10:16,534
actions. And reads the label on
the parcel, which is event data.

147
00:10:16,874 --> 00:10:20,240
Three things can go wrong. The
badge opens too many doors.

148
00:10:20,280 --> 00:10:23,763
The procedure was written by
the person who sent the parcel.

149
00:10:23,803 --> 00:10:27,815
Or the contractor reads the label
aloud as if it were an instruction.

150
00:10:28,155 --> 00:10:32,442
The analogy breaks in one place that
matters: this contractor executes text

151
00:10:32,482 --> 00:10:37,001
literally and instantly, so a label that
contains an instruction is carried out

152
00:10:37,041 --> 00:10:38,443
before anyone can notice.

153
00:10:38,783 --> 00:10:42,976
Analogy for the token. A visitor badge
printed at the start of each shift and

154
00:10:43,016 --> 00:10:47,483
shredded at the end. The desk can print it
with access to one room or to the whole

155
00:10:47,523 --> 00:10:47,978
building.

156
00:10:48,318 --> 00:10:53,252
It breaks because every tool the visitor
picks up can use the badge without asking.

157
00:10:53,292 --> 00:10:58,167
The documentation: "An action can access
the GITHUB_TOKEN through the github.token

158
00:10:58,207 --> 00:11:02,782
context even if the workflow does not
explicitly pass the GITHUB_TOKEN to the

159
00:11:02,822 --> 00:11:03,261
action."

160
00:11:03,601 --> 00:11:07,343
Put the two together and you have
the reason for least privilege.

161
00:11:07,383 --> 00:11:10,775
You don't decide which steps
use the token. Every step can.

162
00:11:10,815 --> 00:11:12,985
You only decide what the token can do.

163
00:11:14,825 --> 00:11:18,867
Try it now, thirty seconds, on
paper. Draw a box for one job.

164
00:11:18,907 --> 00:11:23,015
On its left, write what only
people with write access control.

165
00:11:23,055 --> 00:11:27,766
On its right, what an outsider can
control. For each, who can write it?

166
00:11:27,806 --> 00:11:28,837
Say it out loud.

167
00:11:33,677 --> 00:11:38,715
First the job in the middle, with what it
holds: the token with its permissions, the

168
00:11:38,755 --> 00:11:42,523
secrets named in the job, and an
OIDC token if it has id-token.

169
00:11:42,863 --> 00:11:47,816
Compare with yours. Here the job stands in
the middle, and trust flows in from one

170
00:11:47,856 --> 00:11:52,505
side only. On the left, people with write
access. On the right, anyone on the

171
00:11:52,545 --> 00:11:56,585
internet. Each arrow from the right
is one of the next four videos.

172
00:11:58,425 --> 00:12:02,702
Into the lab. These five files each have
one planted weakness of a different

173
00:12:02,742 --> 00:12:06,450
documented class. In this video
you don't look for the weaknesses.

174
00:12:06,490 --> 00:12:11,051
You take the inventory: for each file, the
trigger, the permissions, the actions,

175
00:12:11,091 --> 00:12:15,708
and the secrets. That's the first half of
the question on screen: what can the job

176
00:12:15,748 --> 00:12:17,185
reach, and what starts it?

177
00:12:17,525 --> 00:12:19,113
Step 1: what is there.

178
00:12:19,453 --> 00:12:25,556
Five files, between 32 and 65 lines. Small
enough to read in full, which the lab

179
00:12:25,596 --> 00:12:26,401
insists on.

180
00:12:26,741 --> 00:12:28,292
Step 2: the triggers.

181
00:12:28,632 --> 00:12:33,537
Five triggers will appear. Which kinds of
event let an outsider start a run, and

182
00:12:33,577 --> 00:12:37,061
which of those run with the
base repository's privileges?

183
00:12:37,101 --> 00:12:38,050
Say it out loud.

184
00:12:42,890 --> 00:12:46,846
issues, opened. pull_request_target.
schedule with a manual trigger.

185
00:12:46,886 --> 00:12:48,433
pull_request. push to main.

186
00:12:51,773 --> 00:12:55,586
Sort them by who can cause the
event. Anyone can open an issue.

187
00:12:55,626 --> 00:13:00,600
Anyone can open a pull request. A push to
main and a manual run need write access,

188
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:05,248
and a schedule needs nobody. And one of
the two pull request triggers is the

189
00:13:05,288 --> 00:13:10,263
privileged one. You haven't found a weakness
yet. You've found where an outsider's

190
00:13:10,303 --> 00:13:11,303
input can arrive.

191
00:13:11,643 --> 00:13:13,308
Step 3: the permissions.

192
00:13:13,648 --> 00:13:18,652
Apply rule one to each block before you
read on. For each file, which scopes does

193
00:13:18,692 --> 00:13:22,202
the token have, and which are
none? Make your prediction.

194
00:13:27,042 --> 00:13:32,368
File 1: issues: write, and therefore
nothing else, not even reading contents.

195
00:13:32,408 --> 00:13:37,594
File 2: read contents, write pull
requests. File 3: write-all, on one line.

196
00:13:37,634 --> 00:13:43,307
Files 4 and 5: read contents. Every file
declares the key, so none of them depends

197
00:13:43,347 --> 00:13:49,021
on the repository's default setting. Whether
each declaration is the least the job

198
00:13:49,061 --> 00:13:50,972
needs is the lab's question.

199
00:13:51,312 --> 00:13:53,056
Step 4: the actions.

200
00:13:53,396 --> 00:13:57,297
Twelve references. Read down
the part after the at sign.

201
00:13:57,337 --> 00:14:01,379
Eleven are 40-character commit
IDs with a version comment.

202
00:14:01,419 --> 00:14:07,219
One line is different. Part five of the
model tells you why that matters, and video

203
00:14:07,259 --> 00:14:08,345
159 is about it.

204
00:14:08,685 --> 00:14:11,049
Step 5: secrets and tokens.

205
00:14:11,389 --> 00:14:16,718
Three lines. Two files hand the job token
to a step through env, its environment

206
00:14:16,758 --> 00:14:22,222
variables, for the GitHub CLI. One file
names a deployment secret, and look at the

207
00:14:22,262 --> 00:14:26,115
line number: 19, near the top
of the file, before any job.

208
00:14:26,155 --> 00:14:31,485
Ask where in a file an env entry applies,
and therefore which jobs and steps can

209
00:14:31,525 --> 00:14:34,908
read that secret. Hold that
question for video 160.

210
00:14:35,248 --> 00:14:39,567
You now have a table with five rows
and four columns, made in one minute.

211
00:14:39,607 --> 00:14:43,269
It tells you where to read. It
doesn't tell you what is wrong.

212
00:14:43,309 --> 00:14:45,537
The lab manual says so in those words.

213
00:14:45,877 --> 00:14:49,633
On your own practice repository.
The interface changes.

214
00:14:49,673 --> 00:14:55,291
The documentation linked from section 21A.3
is the reference, and no GitHub output

215
00:14:55,331 --> 00:15:00,812
was captured by the authors. Lab 29.1
itself needs no GitHub access for its main

216
00:15:00,852 --> 00:15:04,262
part. This is one look that
belongs to this video.

217
00:15:04,602 --> 00:15:09,248
Open the repository's settings for Actions
and find the setting the documentation

218
00:15:09,288 --> 00:15:13,933
calls "Workflow permissions". Read which
of the two options is selected: read and

219
00:15:13,973 --> 00:15:17,462
write for all permissions, or
read for contents and packages.

220
00:15:17,502 --> 00:15:22,263
Don't change anything. Changing a setting
is a decision, and this is an inspection.

221
00:15:22,303 --> 00:15:26,543
Then say aloud what a workflow without
a permissions key would get in this

222
00:15:26,583 --> 00:15:30,708
repository, and why none of the course
workflows depends on that answer.

223
00:15:32,548 --> 00:15:34,196
Five mistakes to watch for.

224
00:15:34,536 --> 00:15:39,942
Relying on the default token permissions.
Root cause: the default is a repository or

225
00:15:39,982 --> 00:15:45,193
organization setting, read-only only for
repositories created since the second of

226
00:15:45,233 --> 00:15:47,851
February 2023; the file does not show it.

227
00:15:48,191 --> 00:15:53,277
Adding one permission and breaking another
step. Root cause: when any permission is

228
00:15:53,317 --> 00:15:56,241
specified, all unspecified
ones are set to none.

229
00:15:56,581 --> 00:16:02,127
permissions: write-all "to make it work".
Root cause: every job, and every action in

230
00:16:02,167 --> 00:16:05,785
every job, can then push,
tag, release and edit issues.

231
00:16:06,125 --> 00:16:10,677
Assuming an action cannot use the token
because the workflow did not pass it.

232
00:16:10,717 --> 00:16:15,091
Root cause: an action can read the
token through the github.token context.

233
00:16:15,431 --> 00:16:20,079
Leaving the checkout credential in place
in a job that only builds and tests.

234
00:16:20,119 --> 00:16:25,133
Root cause: until the job ends, every later
step can run authenticated Git commands

235
00:16:25,173 --> 00:16:27,264
with the job's contents permission.

236
00:16:29,104 --> 00:16:34,090
Now, out of the lab. A team that maintains
an evaluation library has a repository

237
00:16:34,130 --> 00:16:38,991
created in 2021. Its workflows have no
permissions key, because "the default is

238
00:16:39,031 --> 00:16:43,831
read-only now". A new engineer copies
one of those workflows into a repository

239
00:16:43,871 --> 00:16:48,671
created last month, where it behaves
differently: a step that used to create a

240
00:16:48,711 --> 00:16:50,966
release fails for lack of permission.

241
00:16:51,306 --> 00:16:55,331
Nothing in the two files differs.
There's the puzzle from the opening.

242
00:16:55,371 --> 00:17:00,035
What differs is the "Workflow permissions"
setting behind them: permissive in the

243
00:17:00,075 --> 00:17:04,680
old repository, restrictive in the new
one. The engineer has found, by accident,

244
00:17:04,720 --> 00:17:09,326
that the old repository's workflows have
been running with read-write tokens for

245
00:17:09,366 --> 00:17:11,532
years, in every job, for every action.

246
00:17:11,872 --> 00:17:15,821
The fix isn't to switch the new
repository to the permissive option.

247
00:17:15,861 --> 00:17:20,513
It's to declare permissions: contents:
read at the top of every workflow in both

248
00:17:20,553 --> 00:17:24,971
repositories, and to add the one write
scope to the one job that creates the

249
00:17:25,011 --> 00:17:29,722
release. After that, the two repositories
behave the same, and the files say what

250
00:17:29,762 --> 00:17:30,778
the tokens can do.

251
00:17:32,618 --> 00:17:39,961
Your turn. Do Exercise 29.1, "Whose code,
whose text, what can it reach?", in

252
00:17:40,001 --> 00:17:43,604
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

253
00:17:43,944 --> 00:17:48,213
For each job in the exercise, answer the
three parts of the question in writing

254
00:17:48,253 --> 00:17:51,977
before you judge anything: whose
code, whose text, what it can reach.

255
00:17:52,017 --> 00:17:56,340
Predict which jobs will have "somebody
outside the team" in one of the first two

256
00:17:56,380 --> 00:17:56,776
answers.

257
00:17:57,116 --> 00:18:02,396
The challenge is Exercise 29.5, "What
can this token do?", in the same file.

258
00:18:04,236 --> 00:18:07,452
Read the question on screen.
Say your answer out loud.

259
00:18:12,292 --> 00:18:16,934
A strong answer refuses to give one answer
and explains why: it names the setting

260
00:18:16,974 --> 00:18:21,789
that decides, the two options that setting
has, and the date and scope of the change

261
00:18:21,829 --> 00:18:26,645
of default. It says what the token is limited
to in any case, and how long it lives.

262
00:18:26,985 --> 00:18:31,762
Then it gives the recommendation and its
reason. The follow-up adds contents: read

263
00:18:31,802 --> 00:18:36,286
at the top and a labeling job starts
failing with a "not accessible" message.

264
00:18:36,326 --> 00:18:41,103
Explain it with rule one, and give the
smallest correct change, at the right level

265
00:18:41,143 --> 00:18:41,808
of the file.

266
00:18:43,648 --> 00:18:44,575
Let's land this.

267
00:18:44,915 --> 00:18:46,575
You should now be able to say:

268
00:18:46,915 --> 00:18:51,812
The question for every job: whose code runs,
on whose text does it operate, and what

269
00:18:51,852 --> 00:18:52,869
can the job reach?

270
00:18:53,209 --> 00:18:58,013
The model has five parts: the job token,
fork pull requests, privileged triggers,

271
00:18:58,053 --> 00:19:00,524
expression injection, third-party actions.

272
00:19:00,864 --> 00:19:06,060
The job token is created per job, limited
to the workflow's repository, and expires

273
00:19:06,100 --> 00:19:06,880
with the job.

274
00:19:07,220 --> 00:19:12,150
Without a permissions key the token's scope
depends on a repository or organization

275
00:19:12,190 --> 00:19:15,145
setting; naming any scope
sets all others to none.

276
00:19:15,485 --> 00:19:20,923
Declare permissions: contents: read at the
top of every workflow and add scopes per

277
00:19:20,963 --> 00:19:21,187
job.

278
00:19:23,027 --> 00:19:28,026
Read sections 21A.1 to
21A.3 of Chapter 21A.

279
00:19:28,366 --> 00:19:32,547
Today you read five unfamiliar
workflow files and knew where to look.

280
00:19:32,587 --> 00:19:37,687
Try that inventory in the lab. Next time:
fork pull requests, the approval gate, and

281
00:19:37,727 --> 00:19:42,153
privileged triggers. Until then, look
at the state first and type second.

282
00:19:42,193 --> 00:19:43,622
